From dda201f418db2a4f46508645884f641a707908ef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ut-bitra <ut-bitra@localhost>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 09:36:01 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 11/14] arm64: configs: halium: make SELinux the major LSM

SELinux and AppArmor both carry LSM_FLAG_EXCLUSIVE in this tree, and
lsm_allowed() initialises only the first exclusive LSM listed in
CONFIG_LSM. With apparmor listed first, SELinux never initialised and
selinuxfs was never registered.

The Android container needs SELinux. /vendor/bin/vndservicemanager calls
selinux_status_open(true) at startup and aborts without selinuxfs:

  Check failed: selinux_status_open(true ) >= 0

On bitra this produced 32 tombstones in a crash loop. The vndbinder
service table stayed empty, so the QTI composer could not register
display.qservice, and Mir blocked in the HWC2 client until lightdm
killed it after 60 s (black screen).

List selinux before apparmor and set DEFAULT_SECURITY_SELINUX.
SECURITY_SELINUX_DEVELOP keeps the system permissive unless enforcing=1
is given, which is what bring-up needs.

Cost: AppArmor stays compiled in but is never initialised, so the Ubuntu
Touch profiles and click app confinement are not enforced. Booting with
security=apparmor would bring AppArmor back, but it also disables SELinux.

The kernel does not load a policy by itself. Halium replaces Android's
first-stage init, so userspace has to write the vendor
precompiled_sepolicy to /sys/fs/selinux/load (this port does that in
bitra-selinux-policy.service).

Verified on bitra: 44/44 fragment directives present in .config,
selinuxfs registers, vndservicemanager runs, and Lomiri reaches the
language selection screen.
---
 arch/arm64/configs/vendor/halium.config | 49 ++++++++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 35 insertions(+), 14 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/configs/vendor/halium.config b/arch/arm64/configs/vendor/halium.config
index ea68d9d30249..854f15aa25e8 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/configs/vendor/halium.config
+++ b/arch/arm64/configs/vendor/halium.config
@@ -40,21 +40,42 @@ CONFIG_CGROUP_PERF=y
 CONFIG_VT=y
 CONFIG_VT_CONSOLE=y
 
-# --- LSM: AppArmor is the active major LSM -----------------------------------
-# AppArmor and SELinux are both LSM_FLAG_EXCLUSIVE here; the first one listed
-# in the LSM order wins. The order string is set explicitly because a
-# two-step "defconfig + fragment" merge would otherwise keep the
-# selinux-first string computed in the first step.
-# The BPF LSM is deliberately left out of the active list: with AppArmor as
-# the major LSM its no-op stubs become the only implementation of several
-# hooks whose stub default differs from the value security.c expects, e.g.
-# inode_setxattr/inode_removexattr return 0 where 1 is needed to reach the
-# capability check for security.* xattrs. Neither Ubuntu Touch nor the
-# Android container needs BPF LSM programs.
-# Runtime fallback without a rebuild:  lsm=...  or  security=selinux
+# --- LSM: SELinux is the active major LSM -----------------------------------
+# In this tree both SELinux and AppArmor carry LSM_FLAG_EXCLUSIVE
+# (security/selinux/hooks.c, security/apparmor/lsm.c). lsm_allowed() lets only
+# the FIRST exclusive entry in CONFIG_LSM initialise and silently disables any
+# later one, so the order below decides which MAC is live.
+#
+# SELinux must win. The Android container runs /vendor/bin/vndservicemanager,
+# which calls selinux_status_open(true) during startup and abort()s when
+# selinuxfs is missing:
+#     Check failed: selinux_status_open(true ) >= 0
+# Verified on bitra 2026-11-16: with apparmor listed first there is no
+# selinuxfs at all (/proc/filesystems), vndservicemanager crash-looped (32
+# tombstones), the vendor HIDL servicemanager never came up, the display
+# composer never registered its service and Mir blocked in the HWC2 client
+# until lightdm killed it after 60s -> black screen.
+#
+# Cost: AppArmor is compiled in but never initialised, so the Ubuntu Touch
+# profiles in /etc/apparmor.d and click app confinement are not enforced.
+# The container itself runs lxc.apparmor.profile = unconfined either way.
+# Booting with security=apparmor brings AppArmor back but disables SELinux
+# (security= turns off every other legacy-major LSM), which breaks the
+# container again.
+#
+# SELINUX_DEVELOP=y => permissive unless enforcing=1 is given: right for
+# bring-up, and it keeps the container from being killed by an incomplete
+# policy while we still have no working display.
+#
+# The BPF LSM stays out of the list: its no-op stubs change hook defaults
+# (inode_setxattr/inode_removexattr return 0 where 1 is needed to reach the
+# capability check for security.* xattrs).
 CONFIG_SECURITY_APPARMOR=y
-CONFIG_DEFAULT_SECURITY_APPARMOR=y
-CONFIG_LSM="lockdown,yama,loadpin,safesetid,integrity,apparmor,selinux,smack,tomoyo"
+CONFIG_SECURITY_SELINUX=y
+CONFIG_SECURITY_SELINUX_DEVELOP=y
+CONFIG_DEFAULT_SECURITY_SELINUX=y
+# CONFIG_DEFAULT_SECURITY_APPARMOR is not set
+CONFIG_LSM="lockdown,yama,loadpin,safesetid,integrity,selinux,smack,tomoyo,apparmor"
 CONFIG_SECURITY_SELINUX_BOOTPARAM=y
 
 # --- EROFS with LZ4: stock system/vendor/odm/my_* are LZ4-compressed EROFS ---
-- 
2.43.0

